{
  "dom_id": "step-q312581",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{3x-2}{x+2}$ and the point $P(-1,-5)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $(-5,-1)$ is{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(-1,-5).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3x-2}{x+2} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{8}{(x+2)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(-1) & =8\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(-1,-5)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(-5,-1)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(-5) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(-5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
